WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:10.380
So today I'm going to be
talking about Shiny for Python.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:14.060
Without a server running Python.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:17.010
So Shiny for R has been
around for 10 years now.

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:20.600
It's kind of hard to
believe and if you

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.680
want to deploy one of
these applications,

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.300
you have to have a server.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:25.982
Oops whoa.

00:00:25.982 --> 00:00:27.690
Sorry this clicker is
a little sensitive.

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:30.470
You have to have
a server running

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:33.700
R, running Shiny.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.790
Yesterday Joe announced

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.887
Shiny for Python.

00:00:37.887 --> 00:00:39.720
And if you want to
deploy one of these apps,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:45.410
you have to have a server
running Python, running Shiny.

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:48.140
But there's another option where
you can have a server that's

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:50.620
running nothing.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.830
Now, before I go in depth
explaining what that means,

00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:57.950
I want to tell a story about
the early days of Shiny.

00:00:57.950 --> 00:00:59.900
So this was back in 2013.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.950
Shiny had been around
for about a year.

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:04.930
And we had a server
that was available

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.750
where people could
deploy their applications

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:10.660
and people were putting all
sorts of interesting stuff

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:14.110
there because there's
scientists are using R and Shiny

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:17.000
and they're doing all sorts
of cool different things.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.110
One of the applications
that showed up

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:24.160
was a quiz by somebody who was
a graduate student in statistics

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.130
at the time named Josh Katz.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:31.210
So long time users of Shiny
are know what this is about.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.940
This this is one
of the questions

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.350
that he had on the quiz.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:38.740
So, what do you call the insect
that flies around in the summer

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.560
and has a rear section
that glows in the dark?

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:45.310
So you can say you could answer
this lightning bug, firefly,

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:47.870
I use them
interchangeably, or other.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:51.430
So people would go to this,
they'd select their answer,

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:54.980
and it would display a map.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.810
So in the green areas
here, this is where

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:58.530
people tend to say firefly.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:01.010
The blue areas are where people
tend to say lightning bug

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:03.230
and the red areas are
where people use those two

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:05.730
words interchangeably.

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:09.550
All right, let's just
do one more for fun.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:10.780
If I can get it to go.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:14.710
How do you pronounce mayonnaise?

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:16.550
So with three syllables,

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:19.540
may-uh-naze, with two syllables,

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:20.810
man-naze, or

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.800
I use both interchangeably.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:24.310
All right.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.080
So here's the map
in red is where

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.300
people tend to say mayonnaise.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:33.040
And in blue is where people
say mayonnaise and the green

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:35.060
is where people use
them interchangeably.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:37.980
But there doesn't seem
to be a lot on this map.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:38.480
All right.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:42.830
So so you'd answer a
lot of these questions.

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:46.350
And at the end, it would
generate a map for you.

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:51.570
Sorry it would generate
a map for you showing

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:54.935
how similar your personal
dialect is to regional dialects

00:02:54.935 --> 00:02:55.810
in the United States.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:03:01.860
Now, this isn't my map, but this
is actually pretty close to me.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.837
The red is where
your dialect is more

00:03:03.837 --> 00:03:05.170
similar to the regional dialect.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:07.620
So in this case, it's most
similar to Northern California,

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.200
and blue is where
it's least similar.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.750
Now these maps were all
generated dynamically

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:18.190
and that was a little bit
computationally expensive.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:20.770
So but when you're dealing with
four or five people, that's

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:22.728
not, you know, four or
five simultaneous users,

00:03:22.728 --> 00:03:23.770
that's not a big deal.

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:27.760
But this got out
into some I believe

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.560
it got into some
linguistics blog

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:32.620
and then it got picked
up by the media.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:34.673
This is Business Insider.

00:03:34.673 --> 00:03:36.340
There was an article
that ended up here,

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:38.140
and my understanding
actually was

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:40.660
that this was the
most popular article

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:42.160
on their website that year.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.930
So it got picked up here
and some other outlets.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:49.000
And it ended up driving a
lot of traffic to our server.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.240
So at some point we had
thousands of simultaneous users

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:53.770
and our server went
down and we had

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:55.870
to spin up new servers
in AWS to handle

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:58.510
all that computational load.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:01.180
And this was back before we
had these automated scaling

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:03.130
and hosting systems
that we have now.

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:04.600
So that was a very
manual process,

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:05.960
ended up being very expensive.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:08.380
We spent thousands of dollars
running all these servers,

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:11.510
but we survived.

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:14.220
But you know, this raises
an interesting question,

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.980
which is, wouldn't it be nice
if we didn't have to do all

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:20.360
that computation on the server?

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:22.877
So what are some ways of
not doing the computation

00:04:22.877 --> 00:04:23.460
on the server?

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.360
One thing you can do
is maybe you can pre

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:27.240
compute the results.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.560
So sometimes you can do this.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:34.840
And sometimes you can't.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:36.040
Nope sorry.

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.457
Clicker problems here.

00:04:38.457 --> 00:04:39.290
Sometimes you can't.

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:41.890
So some of those maps could
have been pre generated

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:43.810
and actually Josh
changed his app to do that.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:47.940
But that final one with your
personalized map could not be.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:48.440
All right.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:52.353
What about doing
computation on the client?

00:04:52.353 --> 00:04:54.020
Well, you have a
couple of options here.

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:58.060
So one is to do the
computation in the web browser.

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:00.250
But if you know something
about web browsers,

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:05.020
that the only language they
understand is JavaScript.

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:06.370
So that's.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:09.440
And so therefore you must
write your application.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:10.990
Application in JavaScript.

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:13.720
And that can be a problem if
you're doing data science,

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:17.320
because a lot of the
tools that you're used to

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:20.260
might not exist in JavaScript.

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:22.150
So how about another option?

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:24.370
Send the code and
data to the client

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:26.660
and let the user run the
code on their computer.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:28.480
So in order to do
this, though, you

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:32.120
need to duplicate your computing
environment on their computer.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:35.380
And doing this, duplicating
your computing environment

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:37.043
is difficult and
it's a lot of work.

00:05:37.043 --> 00:05:38.710
And you can't reasonably
expect somebody

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:41.950
to take that survey,
that fund survey

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:45.250
and have to install R and/or
Python and a whole bunch

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:46.670
of packages just to do that.

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:54.330
Well, this is actually the story
of how things were back then,

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:56.070
but things have changed.

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:59.250
So this part here
about web browsers

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:01.140
can only run JavaScript.

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:03.270
That's actually no longer true.

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:08.650
And the corollary is
also not true anymore.

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:12.150
So what other language do
web browsers support now?

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.650
Well, there's something called
WebAssembly or it's sometimes

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:17.410
abbreviated WASM.

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:19.750
This is a binary
format for programs

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:22.870
that can run in web browsers.

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:24.870
It's not a language that
you'd write by hand, normally.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:28.390
Usually you'd write code
in C or C++ or some other

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:31.630
languages and compile it to
WebAssembly and then you can

00:06:31.630 --> 00:06:35.730
run it in the browser
at near-native speeds.

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:39.090
So it turns out
Python is written in C

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:42.840
and there's actually a port of
Python called Pyodide which

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:46.170
compiles to WebAssembly and
can run in the web browser.

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:48.660
And of course, I have
to mention webR, which

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:50.820
is a project that
George Stagg started

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:53.470
and is working on, which is
R compiled to WebAssembly.

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:55.053
But this is still
in the early stages,

00:06:55.053 --> 00:06:59.360
so I'm not going to talk more
about it in this presentation.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:01.360
All right, so let's go
back to doing computation

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:03.190
on the client.

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:07.600
So this option 2 here,
this problem here,

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:10.330
duplicating a computing
environment is difficult.

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:12.760
Well, actually, if you compile
your computing environment

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:16.657
to WebAssembly, you can
send it to the web browser.

00:07:16.657 --> 00:07:19.240
And it's actually becomes very
easy to duplicate the computing

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:19.970
environment.

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:21.080
So we'll cross that out.

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:23.770
And then we have this new
combination of options,

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:24.270
one and two.

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:27.700
So we send the code and data
and the computing environment

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:30.040
in WebAssembly to the
client and then run

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:31.440
all of that in the web browser.

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:35.410
All right.

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:37.630
So we have done this with Shiny.

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:42.540
So we've put together Shiny
and Python and WebAssembly

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:44.790
and we put it together
into something

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:47.810
that we are calling ShinyLive.

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:48.510
All right.

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:50.900
So I'm not going to tell
you more a whole lot

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:52.080
more about it right now.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.205
I'm just going to show you
what you can do with it.

00:07:54.205 --> 00:07:56.540
So how many people here have
gone to the documentation

00:07:56.540 --> 00:07:59.690
website for Shiny for Python?

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:03.470
So if you did
that, that's great.

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:05.220
You might have seen
something like this.

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:07.580
So this web page
here and then there's

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:08.830
a running application there.

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:15.630
And I'll just show you
that it's actually running.

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:18.810
Well, this is just a video, but.

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:20.860
So this is a histogram.

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:23.730
You move a slider, and
it regenerates some data

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:26.077
and redraws the histogram.

00:08:26.077 --> 00:08:28.410
So what you might not have
known is that you are already

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:30.960
using Shinylive.

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:33.000
These applications
on the website

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.820
are not hosted on a server
that's running Python.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:38.970
These these examples
are all running inside

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:40.830
of the web browser.

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:45.690
So you can go and you can make
changes to this application.

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:47.250
We'll do it up here.

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:49.270
Let's just change this one.

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:51.750
So it generates a
uniform distribution.

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:54.030
So I'll take out
some of the UI stuff,

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:55.500
make some UI modifications.

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:56.860
Don't worry about the
details about this.

00:08:56.860 --> 00:08:58.527
You just know that
you just have to know

00:08:58.527 --> 00:08:59.730
that I'm changing some code.

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:03.360
And after we make
those changes, then you

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:05.760
can hit the Play
button and you can

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:08.080
see that now it's generating
uniform distribution.

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:12.330
So again, all of that, the
editor and this application

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:14.838
viewer panel in Shiny
itself, all of that

00:09:14.838 --> 00:09:16.380
is running inside
of the web browser.

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:19.210
There's no server
running Python.

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:19.710
All right.

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:22.002
So what are some other fun
things you can do with this?

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:26.250
All right.

00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:27.935
If you start on
the main web page.

00:09:30.752 --> 00:09:32.210
And then there's
an examples button

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:34.125
you can click to see to
browse some examples.

00:09:34.125 --> 00:09:36.500
There's this you don't have
to be able to really read it,

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:39.000
but on the sidebar, there's
some interesting examples there.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:43.320
One of them is a simulation of
the moon orbiting the Earth.

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:43.820
All right.

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:45.110
And I'm just going to.

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:47.600
So what we have here is
the code editor and then

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:49.140
the running
application over there.

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:51.390
And again, this is all running
inside the web browser.

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:55.900
There's no there's no server
in the back running Python.

00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:59.260
So you can make changes to the
initial speed of the Earth.

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:02.380
And then if you do that, you can
see how the moon will follow it

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:03.517
as it orbits.

00:10:03.517 --> 00:10:05.600
But let's say I want to
make some changes to this.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:07.183
This this app is a
little complicated.

00:10:07.183 --> 00:10:08.570
I want to simplify a little bit.

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:13.260
So let's go ahead and
do that really fast.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:18.570
So you can make these
changes right in the browser

00:10:18.570 --> 00:10:21.360
and rerun the application and
try all sorts of new things

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:22.550
out.

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:23.050
All right.

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:24.180
So here we are.

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:26.140
We've simplified the
application a little bit.

00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:28.800
I know it looks pretty similar,
but it is actually simpler.

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:31.890
And this is maybe we've
learned a lot about using Shiny

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:32.970
by doing this.

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:36.100
Now, you might want to share
this with people, right?

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:40.550
Normally to do that,
you would have to

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:43.730
deploy it to some server
that's running Shiny.

00:10:43.730 --> 00:10:46.897
But there's another option
here with Shinylive,

00:10:46.897 --> 00:10:48.480
and I'm going to
zoom in a little bit.

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:49.640
I know this still
may be hard to see,

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:51.450
but I'll show it
even closer later.

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:54.500
So there's a share button and
then when you click on that,

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:57.980
it'll provide these
two sharing links.

00:10:57.980 --> 00:11:00.800
And one of the top
one says Editor URL

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:03.230
and you can copy that URL.

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:05.060
Send it to somebody
and when they paste it

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:08.630
in their web browser,
they'll open it up

00:11:08.630 --> 00:11:10.400
and they will see the
application running

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:11.610
in their browser as well.

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:15.430
And they have that editor panel
and the running application.

00:11:15.430 --> 00:11:16.370
So this is good

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:19.060
if the person you're sending
to is interested in the code

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:21.880
and looking at it
or modifying it.

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:24.980
But maybe the person is
not interested in that.

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:27.950
Maybe they just want to
see the application itself.

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:30.400
So there's another link,
the Application URL,

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:34.520
you can copy that
and send it to them.

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:37.420
And then if they put
that in their browser.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:43.180
It'll open it up and it'll
be running that application

00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:44.145
in the full window.

00:11:46.790 --> 00:11:47.290
All right.

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:52.320
So you might be wondering, like,
hey, how much does this cost?

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:54.000
How do I sign up for this?

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:55.710
And the answer to
those questions

00:11:55.710 --> 00:11:57.252
is, it doesn't cost
anything, and you

00:11:57.252 --> 00:11:59.430
don't have to sign up
for anything to use this.

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:01.610
So let's take a
look at this URL.

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:03.660
This might help explain
things a little bit.

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:05.400
So this is what
the URL looks like.

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:10.185
So it's shinylive.io slash pi
slash editor and then code equals.

00:12:10.185 --> 00:12:11.560
And then there's
this big string.

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:14.400
So that's actually
that string is actually

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:19.140
the whole application
code encoded into the URL.

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:22.230
And when you make a request,
when you send that to somebody

00:12:22.230 --> 00:12:24.340
and they go visit that.

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:28.330
The web browser makes a request
just for this first part,

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:31.090
the part in blue.

00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:32.530
So it requests Shinylive.

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:34.420
Hey, send me all the
Shinylive stuff,

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:38.120
the WebAssembly and
JavaScript stuff.

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:39.030
And Python stuff.

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:42.780
Of course it does not send
this part to the server.

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:44.610
That part is just
in their browser.

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:46.340
And so the browser
then takes Shiny

00:12:46.340 --> 00:12:48.560
Live, decodes the
application, and then

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:50.360
runs it all inside the browser.

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:53.030
So that means that
the server is just

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:55.397
serving these files without
having to think about it.

00:12:55.397 --> 00:12:56.730
It just sends out tons of files.

00:12:56.730 --> 00:13:00.110
And that's really easy
for web servers to do.

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:03.110
Now, you might be
thinking, well,

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:07.790
sharing Shiny apps with
a URL that might be fun,

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:11.420
but maybe the app
and data is too big

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:12.797
to make a reasonably sized URL.

00:13:12.797 --> 00:13:14.880
I mean, browsers can
actually handle from testing.

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:16.880
I found a really large
URLs these days like even

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:19.790
in the megabytes, but that's
that you don't typically

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:25.040
want to do send a huge URL
like that. Maybe it feels weird

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:29.660
just it feels weird to encode
a, an app in a giant URL. That

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:32.400
just seems strange.

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:34.303
Or maybe.

00:13:34.303 --> 00:13:35.720
If I can get this
clicker to work,

00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:38.130
maybe I don't want to depend
on the server at shinyapps

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:40.770
dot io.

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:42.270
Which I don't have
any control over.

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:43.478
Well, I have control over it.

00:13:43.478 --> 00:13:46.240
But you don't.

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:50.650
So another option is to deploy
your Shinylive application.

00:13:50.650 --> 00:13:53.110
So in order to do this,
there's two steps.

00:13:53.110 --> 00:13:57.040
The first is to save your Shiny
live assets and application

00:13:57.040 --> 00:13:58.880
files to a directory.

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:01.250
And then upload that directory
to a web hosting service

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:07.160
like GitHub Pages, or Netlify,
Amazon S3, or CloudFlare pages.
ß
319
00:14:07,160 --&gt; 00:14:10,160
If you're old school, you
can use an Apache web server

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:12.620
or you can deploy it to RStudio
Connect, which is useful

00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:15.042
because it provides
authentication and access

00:14:15.042 --> 00:14:17.000
control if you don't want
the whole world to be

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:19.400
able to see the application.

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:21.230
And let me show
you how this works.

00:14:21.230 --> 00:14:22.180
So it's pretty simple.

00:14:26.130 --> 00:14:26.630
All right.

00:14:26.630 --> 00:14:30.170
So, OK, let's say I have
my application files

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:32.510
in a directory called myapp.

00:14:32.510 --> 00:14:34.490
Now then there's a
command line tool

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:36.990
that comes with Shiny
for Python called Shiny.

00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:39.180
And you say Shiny static

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:40.810
myapp site.

00:14:40.810 --> 00:14:43.940
So site is the directory
that we're writing it to

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:46.640
and it copies it downloads and
copies the Shinylive assets

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:47.700
and puts them in there.

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:50.180
And then it also copies the
application files and puts them

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:52.730
in there.

00:14:52.730 --> 00:14:54.380
And so now we have
a directory, we'll

00:14:54.380 --> 00:14:57.710
take a look here directory
called site and then

00:14:57.710 --> 00:14:59.780
and then we can test,
Hey can actually

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:03.380
serve this site
directory and, and have

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:06.770
people run my application?

00:15:06.770 --> 00:15:09.110
So we'll just run this
simple web server,

00:15:09.110 --> 00:15:11.930
Python web server and
look at visit local host

00:15:11.930 --> 00:15:16.000
and then it will load the
application right here.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:16.500
All right.

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:19.125
Now, at this point, this is when
you can take that set of files

00:15:19.125 --> 00:15:21.520
and just upload it to your
static web hosting service,

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:24.620
whatever that may be.

00:15:24.620 --> 00:15:27.610
OK let's take a look at
the deployment model here.

00:15:27.610 --> 00:15:29.360
So for a traditional
Shiny app deployment,

00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:32.090
you have a server that's
running R or running Python.

00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:33.625
And computers connect to it.

00:15:33.625 --> 00:15:35.000
And when they
connect to it, they

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:36.830
maintain this open connection.

00:15:36.830 --> 00:15:38.730
The user interacts
with it a little bit.

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:41.313
It sends data to the server, the
server does some computation,

00:15:41.313 --> 00:15:44.260
and it sends some
information back.

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:45.920
For Shinylive app deployment

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:49.330
you have a server that
doesn't run R or Python.

00:15:49.330 --> 00:15:52.390
When the user connects
to it, it sends

00:15:52.390 --> 00:15:58.290
it sends Python
and the app code.

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:02.250
And Shiny, of course, to those
computers, and all of that stuff

00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:04.180
runs inside of those
client computers.

00:16:04.180 --> 00:16:07.115
And it's very easy for a server
to just send those files.

00:16:07.115 --> 00:16:09.240
That's that's not a
computationally intensive thing

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:10.050
to do.

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:12.770
So it can serve you can serve
a lot of users this way.

00:16:15.192 --> 00:16:17.150
OK let's talk about the
benefits of Shinylive.

00:16:17.150 --> 00:16:18.962
To summarize, so the
entire application

00:16:18.962 --> 00:16:20.670
runs on the client
instead of the server,

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:23.970
so there's no computational
load on the server.

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:27.270
It's really easy to
share these applications.

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:30.300
It's easy to deploy them to
a static web hosting service,

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:33.540
and it's easy to scale
them because that's

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:35.710
what these static web
hosting services do.

00:16:35.710 --> 00:16:37.860
That's that's their
bread and butter.

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:39.940
And it's very cheap.

00:16:39.940 --> 00:16:42.490
And it works on devices
without Python installed.

00:16:42.490 --> 00:16:45.308
So like Chromebooks
or iPads or phones.

00:16:45.308 --> 00:16:47.350
And this is not just for
viewing the application,

00:16:47.350 --> 00:16:50.110
but you can even
edit the applications

00:16:50.110 --> 00:16:53.620
and play with them on there.

00:16:53.620 --> 00:16:56.192
So it's not

00:16:56.192 --> 00:16:57.650
it's not all sunshine
and rainbows.

00:16:57.650 --> 00:16:59.980
There's also a few
drawbacks and limitations.

00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:04.400
So one is that not all packages
are available in Pyodide.

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:06.700
So Pyodide contains a lot
of packages in the Python

00:17:06.700 --> 00:17:11.140
scientific stack, including
pandas and matplotlib, but not

00:17:11.140 --> 00:17:12.819
all packages for Python.

00:17:12.819 --> 00:17:15.310
You can install
packages from PyPi

00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:17.050
if they're written
in pure Python

00:17:17.050 --> 00:17:18.730
and don't have
any compiled code.

00:17:18.730 --> 00:17:20.680
So but hopefully,
hopefully this situation

00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:21.805
will improve in the future.

00:17:21.805 --> 00:17:24.550
But that's what it is right now.

00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:26.230
The code and data
for the application

00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:27.829
is sent to the
web, web browser.

00:17:27.829 --> 00:17:29.495
So there's no secrets.

00:17:29.495 --> 00:17:31.870
Somebody can, in principle,
look at all the code and data

00:17:31.870 --> 00:17:35.760
for your application if
they can see your app.

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:37.540
It's not good for
large amounts of data.

00:17:37.540 --> 00:17:39.207
Like if you've got
100 megabytes of data

00:17:39.207 --> 00:17:41.670
that your application
is operating on,

00:17:41.670 --> 00:17:43.930
you probably don't want
to send that to somebody's

00:17:43.930 --> 00:17:45.660
web browser, that's a lot.

00:17:45.660 --> 00:17:49.180
It's not unheard of,
but that is a lot.

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:52.870
So the download side so download
size for a basic application,

00:17:52.870 --> 00:17:57.110
the browser will download
about 13 megabytes of stuff.

00:17:57.110 --> 00:18:01.550
And if you're loading packages
from there like, like pandas,

00:18:01.550 --> 00:18:03.110
it's just going to
go up from there.

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:07.770
But fortunately browser
caching will speed up

00:18:07.770 --> 00:18:09.000
subsequent visits.

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:11.440
So the first time somebody
views it, it might be slow.

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:12.360
And especially in
this conference,

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:13.800
Wi-Fi the first time
you visit one of these

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:16.110
may be a little bit slow,
but then in later visits

00:18:16.110 --> 00:18:19.380
it should be
really, really fast.

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:21.090
And finally, the
browser security sandbox

00:18:21.090 --> 00:18:24.450
restricts network connectivity.

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:26.598
But the good news is,
if Shinylive doesn't

00:18:26.598 --> 00:18:28.140
work for your
application, you always

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:30.600
have the option to do
a traditional Shiny app

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:31.180
deployment.

00:18:31.180 --> 00:18:33.210
You can learn this
one framework Shiny

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:36.183
and when it's appropriate,
deploy it with Shinylive.

00:18:36.183 --> 00:18:38.100
And if that doesn't work
for you can deploy it

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:43.100
with a traditional deployment
just by learning one framework.

00:18:43.100 --> 00:18:45.900
OK so this is where you
can learn more about it.

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:48.520
shiny.rstudio.com slash py

00:18:48.520 --> 00:18:51.680
There's a link at the
top for Shinylive.

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:53.210
So also, it's.

00:18:53.210 --> 00:18:55.160
It's still experimental.

00:18:55.160 --> 00:18:56.250
Please keep that in mind.

00:18:56.250 --> 00:18:57.750
We're still learning
a lot about it.

00:18:57.750 --> 00:19:00.687
And so we can't wait
to see what you're

00:19:00.687 --> 00:19:03.020
going to do with it, because
we want to learn what kinds

00:19:03.020 --> 00:19:06.710
of possibilities people are,
what, what the possibilities

00:19:06.710 --> 00:19:09.750
are, and hopefully you'll
be able to teach us that.

00:19:09.750 --> 00:19:13.170
And of course, I know everyone
is thinking about this.

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:15.460
Yes we're hoping to do
this with Shiny for R.

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:17.400
Ah, but there's a
lot of work that

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:19.800
needs to be done before we
can get started on this.

00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:21.620
Thank you.

